I think we should. People confuse words in natlangs too, and there are=20
questions on proficiency tests for there/they're/their and lay/lie (the pas=
t=20
of one verb is homophonous with the present of the other). One pair that=20
trips me up in Spanish is vendr=E1/vender=E1, because French vendra is Span=
ish=20
vender=E1 and French viendra is Spanish vendr=E1.

I thought of another one. A sentence I made up for illustrating "be'o" is "=
ti=20
zbasu be lo takybli bei lo kliti be'o minji". Remove "be'o" and it's still=
=20
grammatical, but silly: "This makes bricks out of clay machines." As a test=
=20
question:

ti<u> </u>zbasu <u>be</u> lo takybli <u>bei</u> lo kliti<u> </u>minji
A. <u> </u>
B. <u>be</u>
C. <u>bei</u>
D. <u> </u>
E. <i>no se srera</i>

Is that a good way of indicating a missing word?

